\[
(10)\qquad\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
E_{U_S} \arrow[d] \& E_{U_{O_x}} \arrow[l] \arrow[d] \& E_{U_K} \arrow[l] \arrow[d] \\
E_S \& E_{\underline O_x} \arrow[l] \& E_K \arrow[l] \arrow[ul,dashed,"\varphi_x"]
\end{tikzcd}}
\]
with cartesian squares, and surjective horizontal arrows. The homomorphism $E_K\longleftarrow E_{U_S}$ is the composite of a lifting $E_K-E_{U_{O_x}}$ of $E_K\longrightarrow E_{O_x}$, with the canonical homomorphism $\unsure{\widehat E}_{U_{O_x}}-E_{U_S}$. (The \inserted{\deleted{latter}} liftings $E_K\longrightarrow E_{U_{O_x}}$ correspond bijectively$^{\mathrm t}$ to the sections of $E_{U_\eta}$ over $E_K$, or to the lifting$^{\mathrm s}$ of $E_K-E_S$ to $E_K-E_{U_S}$\ldots)

That said, I feel like proving that $\varphi_x:E_K\longrightarrow E_{U_{O_x}}$ vanishes \unsure{on|in} $I_x$, that is, comes from a section of $E_{U_{O_x}}$ over $\underline O_x$ if and only if the corresponding$^{\mathrm t}$ rational section * of $U_S/S$ is defined at $x$. This \unsure{will imply|implies}
\margin{\uline{Note well} The quotient of $E_K$ by the \unsure{subgroup} generated by $\unsure{I_x|\overline I_x}$ is none other than $E_{\underline O_x}=\pi_1(\operatorname{Spec}\unsure{O_x})$}
\margin{72}
